===persian===

هوای ابر را بلبل نداره
به جز شوق گل و سنبل نداره
نمی‌دونه اگه بارون نباره
گل پژمرده بوی گل نداره

۱۳۸۰

===machine===

The nightingale has no longing for the cloud
- only desire for the rose and hyacinth.
It doesn't know: if the rain doesn't fall,
the wilted flower has no flower's scent...

1380

===lantern===

The nightingale has no use for clouds
just the rose and hyacinth.
It doesn't know: if the rain won't fall,
the wilted flower has no scent...

===translation===

Nightingale couldn't weather a cloud
he only ever took a shine to hyacinth & rose
he doesn't know - if the rain won't rain,
the wilted rose is but a scentless rose.

===footnotes===

The Persian is a do-baytī — a folk quatrain in colloquial speech (نداره، بارون), the register of Bābā Tāher's songs — and its first line turns on هوا: at once "weather" and "craving." The nightingale has no *havā* for clouds. No single English word holds both senses, so this translation scatters the pun across three — "weather," "took a shine," "rain won't rain." Read it aloud with a slight Irish lilt: Hiberno-English keeps "took a shine to" and "is but" in one living voice, much as the original's dialect carries its old forms unselfconsciously.
